34. PUBLIC HEARING regarding the CK Gold project site plan located on
portions of Sects 25 and 36, T14N, R70W, and portions of Sects 28,
29 and 32, and all of Sect 31, T14N, R69W, Laramie County, WY.
25 - 266
CK Gold Project Site Plan
Attachments:
Justin Arnold, Planning, said Gold King Corporation applied for approval of the CK
Gold Project Site Plan located on the south half of Sect 25 and all of Sect 36, T14N,
R70W, and portions of Sect 28, 29, and 32 and all of Sect 31, T14N, R69W. The
proposed site is located off Rd 210 approximately 20 miles west of Cheyenne.
He noted the project is the development of a gold and copper mine that will use an
open pit, processing plant, waste rock facilities, and a tailings management facility.
The property is owned by both private parties and the State of Wyoming. Access will
be via a gravel road to Rd 210 approximately two miles west of Happy Jack Rd. He
added the access road easement will be fenced on both sides and the project will
also be fenced.
Kevin Francis, agent for the applicant, made a presentation about the project
describing construction and operations, anticipated impacts, mitigating measures for
noise and dust, and estimated revenue for the state and county.  Francis said it is
possible the open pit could be re-purposed as a water reservoir when the mine
closes.
Arnold presented the staff report and said based on evidence provided, staff
recommend approval of the CK Gold Project site plan with one condition and
adoption of findings of fact a and b of the staff report.
Chairman Malm opened the hearing for PUBLIC COMMENT.
Resident Jeremy Eller-McNeely spoke in opposition to project, citing environmental
concerns for water resources and wildlife, community health, and negative impact on
tourism and recreation integrity of Curt Gowdy.
Chairman Malm said many of residents' concerns are addressed in the Industrial
Siting process and are not things that can be imposed on the applicant through land
use regulations or the site plan process.
Francis said most items are addressed through DEQ's mine operating permitting
process, and they have also gone through US Fish and Wildlife Service.  Water
Page 12 of 15
```

