```text
Board of County Commissioners
Minutes of the Proceedings - Final
June 17, 2025
Powerhouse Mountain Cell Tower Site Plan
Powerhouse Mountain Cell Tower Site Plan, Handout
Attachments:
Justin Arnold, Planning, said Rocky Mountain Towers, on behalf of Mike and Lorinda
Zumo, applied for approval of the Powderhouse Mountain Cell Tower site plan located
in a portion of Sect 4, T14N, R66W, Laramie County. The proposed site is located at
2409 Iron Mountain Rd. The project is the construction of a multicarrier wireless
communication facility and proposes a 150-foot monopole and ground-mounted
telecommunications equipment within an 80 foot x 80 foot fenced leased area. The
fenced area will accommodate the ground equipment cabinets and utilities of its
anchor tenant and other future subtenants.
Arnold said based on evidence provided, staff recommend approval of the
Powderhouse Mountain Cell Tower Site Plan with one condition and adoption of the
findings of fact a, b, and c of the staff report.
Shelly Neace, agent for the applicant and owner of Rocky Mountain Towers, made a
presentation summarizing the project and the need to expand and improve
telecommunications coverage.
Chairman Malm opened the hearing for PUBLIC COMMENT.
Joseph Perry, Patrick Nealon, Cindy Braden, Lucie Osborn, Galen Styskal, Dwayne
Lewis, Jeremy Eller-McNeely, Ken Roylance, and Chris Roth were among residents
speaking in opposition to the project.  Their concerns and issues included decline in
property values, health risks, radiofrequency emissions, project does not fit in with
rural residential setting, view pollution, applicability of zoning regulations, and
opposition from majority of residents. Petitions and correspondence objecting to the
project were also presented to the board.
Neace addressed residents' concerns and stated the industry is heavily regulated by
numerous federal and state agencies and the proposed tower site is in the best
location.
Hearing no further PUBLIC COMMENT, Chairman Malm closed the hearing.
Chairman Malm asked Arnold about regulations on wireless telecommunication
services.  Arnold said wireless telecommunications services are not included in the
zone districts but are in a standalone section of Laramie County Land Use
Regulations.
Commissioners Heath, Hollingshead, and Zwonitzer noted need for improved and
reliable communications for public safety and emergencies.
A motion was made by Commissioner Heath, seconded by Commissioner
Hollingshead, to approve the site plan for the Powderhouse Mountain Cell
